IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (Western Australia) -- commencement (s 2 and the compilation table); notifying
-- and registering a birth (ss 12, 13, 15, 16), parentage details (s 18), a different surname (s 19(3), (4)) and the child's name
-- (s 22); registering a marriage (Part 6, ss 37-38); and notifying, certifying and registering a death (Part 7, ss 40, 42, 44, 45,
-- 47, 48).
--
-- Edition: compilation including the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Amendment (Sex or Gender Changes) Act 2024 Pt 2 (in
-- force 30 May 2025), the last commenced amendment in its compilation table; "(c) State of Western Australia 2025"; as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49099.txt (retrieved 2026-09-07). The deposit states no compilation number. The Assisted
-- Reproductive Technology and Surrogacy Act 2025 Pt 15 is listed as uncommenced and is not shown.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement au/wa:REQ-0001 in subjects/au/wa/requirements.jsonl: priority 1, raised 12 times by the three Western
-- Australian cradle-to-grave lives (chloe c001, c054, c062, c071, c086, c096, c098; jake k001, k051, k097; daniel d001, d092). They
-- ask: who must register a birth, and by when; may both unmarried parents be registered; what of the child's name; what registers a
-- marriage; who registers a death, and by when. SCOPED to those questions, with the definitions and exceptions needed to answer them.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the Registrar and administration (Part 2); court orders (ss 20-21, 41) beyond their effect as an input; given-name
-- changes (s 23); parentage changes, adoption and surrogacy (Part 4); change of name (Part 5); change of registration of sex or
-- gender (Part 5A); suspected deaths (s 43); disposal out of the State (s 46); the Register, access and certificates (Part 8);
-- offences and miscellany (Part 9); fees and approved forms (set by regulations or the Registrar; none was retrieved). Where a
-- question below can REACH one of these, it answers `not covered`, naming the gap, rather than answering (see `Why not covered`).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent
-- test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.
-- Conformed to the writing-l4-rules skill on 14 October 2026, and to section 5 of docs/ENCODING-GUIDE.md (what the stable l4,
-- 1.6.98, accepts) on 22 October 2026.
--
-- THE CLOCK. Every WITHIN in this module, and every AT in a #TRACE of a rule in it, counts DAYS -- and only this comment says so.
-- AT 0 is the day of the event the period runs from (the birth; the death, finding or placement of the body; the disposal), and an
-- act stamped n happened n days after it. "Within N days after" is WITHIN N, read inclusively: an act on day N is in time, on day
-- N + 1 it is late (the first pass read s 16 the same way: "A statement lodged on day 60 is in time"). The doctor's 48 hours under
-- s 44 are on a clock of HOURS and so live in their own module, bdm-cause-of-death.l4.

§ `Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998`

§§ `Dates, and the questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4's daydate has no YMD, so this is a local shim (docs/ENCODING-GUIDE.md section 5). daydate's `Date` takes the day,
-- the month and the year, in that order. Defined here once; bdm-cause-of-death.l4 and the case file have it by IMPORT.
GIVEN `the year`  IS A NUMBER
      `the month` IS A NUMBER
      `the day`   IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
YearMonthDay `the year` `the month` `the day` MEANS Date `the day` `the month` `the year` -- renamed from YMD so it cannot collide with the YMD in newer daydate libraries; see docs/ENCODING-GUIDE.md section 5

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the answer, or
-- `not covered` with the reason, which is one of these. Each reason is the message of the named refusal it replaces.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 2; the compilation table, Gazette 9 Apr 1999 p 1433: the law in force before 14 April 1999 is not deposited
    `a registrable event before 14 April 1999 is governed by the law then in force, which is not encoded in this model`
    -- s 38, note: Marriage Act 1961 (Cth) s 50(4)
    `the celebrant's duty to lodge the certificate under the Marriage Act 1961 (Cth) s 50(4) is not encoded in this model`
    -- s 43
    `a suspected death established at an inquest, where the coroner is the notifying person under s 43, is not encoded in this model`
    -- s 42(d), s 44(5)
    `what s 42(d) requires for a death reportable to a coroner, which s 44(5) exempts from the certificate, is not encoded in this model`
    -- s 46
    `notice of a removal of remains from the State under s 46 is not encoded in this model`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `The parties and the acts`

-- The offices and persons on whom ss 12, 15, 16, 18, 19, 22, 42, 44, 45, 47 and 48 put a duty or a power. Each is an office or a
-- role, never a named person (the phrasebook, entry 6.9). `another person` is anyone the Act puts no duty on -- s 12(3)'s "another
-- person", s 44(4)(a)'s "another doctor".
DECLARE `A party under this Act` IS ONE OF
    `the chief executive of the health service provider`
    `the hospital's chief executive officer or general manager`
    `the doctor or midwife caring for the mother`
    `the doctor who examined the stillborn child`
    `any person in attendance at the birth`
    `both parents jointly`
    `the foundling's long-term carer`
    `the Registrar`
    `the funeral director or other person arranging disposal`
    `the person in charge of the institution`
    `the funeral director or other person with custody of the remains`
    `the doctor responsible under section 44`
    `another person`

DECLARE `An act under this Act` IS ONE OF
    `give the Registrar notice of the birth`
    `lodge the birth registration statement`
    `accept the birth registration statement`
    `register the birth`
    `include information about the identity of the child's parents in the Register`
    `change the surname entered in the Register to the proposed surname`
    `assign a name to the child`
    `lodge a death registration statement`
    `give the Registrar the certificate of cause of death`
    `certify the cause of the death`
    `give the certificate to the person responsible under section 42`
    `give the Registrar notice of the disposal`
    `give the Registrar notice that the remains have not been disposed of`
    `register the death`

§§ `The facts of a case`

-- s 12(1)(a): the hospital where the child was born or to which it was brought within 24 hours after the birth; `no hospital`
-- otherwise. Which hospitals are public (Health Services Act 2016 s 6) or private (Private Hospitals and Health Services Act 1927
-- s 2(1)) is a fact the caller supplies.
DECLARE Hospital IS ONE OF
    `a public hospital`
    `a private hospital`
    `no hospital`

DECLARE Birth HAS
    `date of the birth`                                                        IS A DATE
    `in the State`                                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `hospital involved`                                                        IS A Hospital
    `a stillbirth`                                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `a doctor or midwife in attendance`                                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `registration ordered by a State court`                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `in an aircraft or on a vessel on the way to the State`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `outside the Commonwealth, the child to become a resident of the State`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `registered under a corresponding law`                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `a foundling`                                                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `a neonatal death`                                                         IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 4 "disposal": (a) cremation; (b) burial (including burial at sea); (c) placing in a mausoleum or other permanent resting place;
-- (d) removal from the State.
DECLARE Disposal IS ONE OF
    `cremation`
    `burial`
    `placing in a mausoleum or other permanent resting place`
    `removal of the remains from the State`

DECLARE Death HAS
    `date of the death`                                                        IS A DATE
    `in the State`                                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `registration ordered by a State court or coroner`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the death of a stillborn child`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the remains placed for medical education or research`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `reportable to a coroner under section 17(3) of the Coroners Act 1996`     IS A BOOLEAN
    `a suspected death established at an inquest`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `a coronial investigation into the cause incomplete`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `date of the disposal`                                                     IS A MAYBE DATE

§§ `2 -- commencement`

-- s 2: "This Act comes into operation on such day as is fixed by proclamation." Compilation table: "Act other than s. 1 and 2: 14 Apr
-- 1999 (see s. 2 and Gazette 9 Apr 1999 p. 1433)". Before that day the Act says nothing about an event; the law then in force does.
-- That is the law's own answer, so it is a value (the phrasebook, entry 4.1). What that earlier law required is the encoder's gap,
-- so a duty asked about such an event is `not covered` (entry 11.5).
DECLARE `The operation of this Act` IS ONE OF
    `the Act was not yet in operation`
    `the Act was in operation`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 2; compilation table, Gazette 9 Apr 1999 p 1433
GIVEN `the date of the event` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `The operation of this Act`
`the operation of the Act on` `the date of the event` MEANS
    IF   `the date of the event` AT LEAST YearMonthDay 1999 4 14
    THEN `the Act was in operation`
    ELSE `the Act was not yet in operation`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 2
GIVEN `the date of the event` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the event was before the Act was in operation` `the date of the event` IF
    `the operation of the Act on` `the date of the event` EQUALS `the Act was not yet in operation`

§§ `12 -- notification of a birth`

-- s 12(1) "responsible person": (a) if the child "was born in a hospital or brought to a hospital within 24 hours after the child's
-- birth" -- (i) a public hospital: "the chief executive of the health service provider for that hospital"; (ii) a private hospital:
-- "the chief executive officer or general manager of the hospital"; (b) in any other case -- (i) "the doctor or midwife responsible
-- for the professional care of the child's mother at the birth"; (ii) if stillborn, "the doctor who examined the child's body after
-- the birth"; (iii) if no doctor or midwife attended, "any person in attendance at the birth".
-- s 12(2): notice "within one month after the birth" in an approved form. Penalty: $1 000. (3): no offence if another person gives it.
--
-- Limb (b)(i) is the default of (b), so it is the OTHERWISE arm; (b)(ii) is tried before (b)(iii), so a stillborn child born with
-- nobody qualified in attendance falls to (b)(ii). The Act does not order (ii) and (iii); this order is the encoder's.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 12(1)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A `A party under this Act`
`the person responsible for notifying the birth` b MEANS
    CONSIDER b's `hospital involved`
    WHEN `a public hospital`  THEN `the chief executive of the health service provider`
    WHEN `a private hospital` THEN `the hospital's chief executive officer or general manager`
    WHEN `no hospital`        THEN
        BRANCH
            IF   b's `a stillbirth`
            THEN `the doctor who examined the stillborn child`
            IF   NOT (b's `a doctor or midwife in attendance`)
            THEN `any person in attendance at the birth`
            OTHERWISE `the doctor or midwife caring for the mother`

-- "within one month after the birth": a calendar month, reached with `add months`, which clamps to the last day of a shorter month
-- (31 January -> 28 February). No interpretation Act was consulted; this is an inference, as it was in the first pass.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 12(2)(a)
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`one month after` `the date of birth` MEANS
    `add months` `the date of birth` 1

-- The same month, as a count of days on this module's clock, for the WITHIN below.
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days in the month after the birth` b MEANS
    Day (`one month after` (b's `date of the birth`)) MINUS Day (b's `date of the birth`)

-- THE DUTY. "When a child is born in the State, the responsible person must give the Registrar notice of the birth -- (a) within one
-- month after the birth". A birth outside the State puts no duty on anyone under s 12.
-- This is the duty as s 12(2) states it, the rule a #TRACE runs. It does not look at the date: for a birth before 14 April 1999 the
-- question is the next rule's, which answers `not covered`.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 12(2)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 12(2) -- the responsible person must give the Registrar notice of the birth within one month` b MEANS
    IF   b's `in the State`
    THEN PARTY  `the person responsible for notifying the birth` b
         MUST   `give the Registrar notice of the birth`
         WITHIN `the number of days in the month after the birth` b
         HENCE  FULFILLED
         LEST   BREACH BY (`the person responsible for notifying the birth` b)
                BECAUSE "s 12(2): the responsible person did not give the Registrar notice of the birth within one month after the birth"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- THE QUESTION. The s 12(2) duty for this birth, or why this encoding does not cover it.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 2, s 12(2)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH AN `Answer` (DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`)
`s 12(2) -- the duty to notify the birth, or why it is not covered` b MEANS
    IF   `the event was before the Act was in operation` (b's `date of the birth`)
    THEN `not covered` `a registrable event before 14 April 1999 is governed by the law then in force, which is not encoded in this model`
    ELSE `answered` (`s 12(2) -- the responsible person must give the Registrar notice of the birth within one month` b)

-- THE OFFENCE. Whether failing the duty is an offence is constitutive, and s 12(3) takes the offence away -- not the duty -- when
-- someone else gave the notice (the phrasebook, entry 5.7). The penalty ceiling is in `Penalties` below.
DECLARE `Notice of the birth` HAS
    `date of the notice`        IS A DATE
    `given by another person`   IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 12(2), (3)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
      n IS A `Notice of the birth`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the responsible person commits an offence under s 12(2)` b n IF
        b's `in the State`
    AND n's `date of the notice` GREATER THAN `one month after` (b's `date of the birth`)
    AND NOT (n's `given by another person`)

§§ `13 -- when a birth must or may be registered`

-- s 13(1): "If a child is born in the State, the birth must be registered under this Act." (2): also if a State court so orders.
-- (3) another State's or the Commonwealth's court determination: MAY, if the Registrar considers it appropriate. (4) born in an
-- aircraft or vessel on the way to the State, and (5) born outside the Commonwealth to become a resident of the State: MAY.
-- (6): "The Registrar must not register a birth under subsection (4) or (5) if the birth is registered under a corresponding law."
--
-- (1) and (2) name no party: "must be registered" is a condition later rules consult, not a duty (the phrasebook, entry 5.2); the
-- duty to have it registered is s 16's. (4) and (5) are the passive "may be registered" of entry 5.1, an eligibility test. Only (6)
-- names a party, the Registrar, and forbids an act: a prohibition, with no period, so no WITHIN (entry 5.3).
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 13(1), (2)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the birth must be registered` b IF
        "(1)" ... b's `in the State`
    OR  "(2)" ... b's `registration ordered by a State court`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 13(4), (5)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the birth is within s 13(4) or (5)` b IF
        "(4)" ... b's `in an aircraft or on a vessel on the way to the State`
    OR  "(5)" ... b's `outside the Commonwealth, the child to become a resident of the State`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 13(4)-(6)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the birth may be registered under s 13(4) or (5)` b IF
        `the birth is within s 13(4) or (5)` b
    AND "(6)" ... NOT (b's `registered under a corresponding law`)

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 13(6)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 13(6) -- the Registrar must not register the birth` b MEANS
    IF       `the birth is within s 13(4) or (5)` b
         AND b's `registered under a corresponding law`
         AND NOT (`the birth must be registered` b)
    THEN PARTY `the Registrar`
         SHANT `register the birth`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST  BREACH BY `the Registrar`
               BECAUSE "s 13(6): the Registrar registered under s 13(4) or (5) a birth already registered under a corresponding law"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `15, 16 -- who must have the birth registered, and by when`

-- s 15(1): "The parents of a child are jointly responsible for having the child's birth registered ... (and must both sign the birth
-- registration statement)" but the Registrar may accept one parent's statement if joining the other is impracticable "because of his
-- or her death, disappearance, ill health or unavailability or the need to avoid unwarranted distress". (2): a foundling -- "the
-- person with responsibility for the long-term care, welfare and development of the child". Nothing in s 15 turns on whether the
-- parents are married.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 15(1), (2)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A `A party under this Act`
`who is responsible for registering the birth` b MEANS
    IF   b's `a foundling`
    THEN `the foundling's long-term carer`
    ELSE `both parents jointly`

DECLARE `One-parent statement` HAS
    `the other parent's death, disappearance, ill health or unavailability`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the need to avoid unwarranted distress in obtaining both signatures`      IS A BOOLEAN

-- Whether the Registrar MAY accept one parent's statement is a test (entry 5.1); that the Registrar may then do it is the permission
-- (entry 5.4). The Act sets the Registrar no period, so the permission carries no WITHIN.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 15(1)
GIVEN s IS A `One-parent statement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Registrar may accept one parent's statement` s IF
        s's `the other parent's death, disappearance, ill health or unavailability`
    OR  s's `the need to avoid unwarranted distress in obtaining both signatures`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 15(1)
GIVEN s IS A `One-parent statement`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 15(1) -- the Registrar may accept the statement of one parent` s MEANS
    IF   `the Registrar may accept one parent's statement` s
    THEN PARTY `the Registrar`
         MAY   `accept the birth registration statement`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- s 16(1): the responsible person "must ensure that a birth registration statement is lodged with the Registrar within 60 days after
-- the date of the birth". Penalty: $1 000. (2): "The Registrar must accept a birth registration statement even though it is lodged
-- after the end of the 60 day period." A statement lodged on day 60 is in time.
--
-- The duty falls on the person "responsible under section 15(1) or (2)". s 15 is not confined to births in the State, but outside
-- s 13(1) and (2) registration is only permitted, never required; this encoding takes the duty to bite only where s 13 requires
-- registration. The joint parents are one party here: s 15(1)'s "must both sign" is a condition of the statement, not a second
-- duty, and is not separately modelled. This is the duty as s 16(1) states it, the rule a #TRACE runs; the date is the next rule's.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 16(1)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 16(1) -- the responsible person must have the statement lodged within 60 days` b MEANS
    IF   `the birth must be registered` b
    THEN PARTY  `who is responsible for registering the birth` b
         MUST   `lodge the birth registration statement`
         WITHIN 60
         HENCE  FULFILLED
         LEST   BREACH BY (`who is responsible for registering the birth` b)
                BECAUSE "s 16(1): the birth registration statement was not lodged within 60 days after the date of the birth"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- THE QUESTION. As for s 12(2): the duty above, or `not covered` for a birth before 14 April 1999.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 2, s 16(1)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH AN `Answer` (DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`)
`s 16(1) -- the duty to have the birth registered, or why it is not covered` b MEANS
    IF   `the event was before the Act was in operation` (b's `date of the birth`)
    THEN `not covered` `a registrable event before 14 April 1999 is governed by the law then in force, which is not encoded in this model`
    ELSE `answered` (`s 16(1) -- the responsible person must have the statement lodged within 60 days` b)

-- s 16(2) is not a cure for s 16(1): a statement lodged on day 61 is still a breach of (1), and the Registrar must still accept it.
-- So it is a duty of its own, on the Registrar, with no period (entry 5.9), and its trace starts at the lodgement. The boolean says
-- the same thing for any lodgement on or after the birth, however late.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 16(2)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 16(2) -- the Registrar must accept the statement even though it is lodged late` MEANS
    PARTY `the Registrar`
    MUST  `accept the birth registration statement`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the Registrar`
          BECAUSE "s 16(2): the Registrar did not accept a birth registration statement lodged after the end of the 60 day period"

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 16(2)
GIVEN `the day of lodgement` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Registrar must accept a statement lodged on day` `the day of lodgement` IF
    `the day of lodgement` AT LEAST 0

§§ `18 -- the parents' identity in the Register`

-- s 18(1): the Registrar "is not to include information about the identity of a child's parents" unless (a) "both parents of the
-- child make a joint application"; (b) one parent applies and the other "cannot join" -- dead, cannot be found, or some other
-- reason; (c) one parent applies and the Registrar is satisfied the other "does not dispute the correctness"; (d) a State court orders
-- it or finds parentage; (e) another court so determines and the Registrar thinks it appropriate; (f) the Registrar may presume
-- parentage under any law. (2)(c): one parent's own identity may be included on that parent's own statement. Marriage is not a
-- condition anywhere in s 18. (d) and (e) are one input here, as in the first pass.
DECLARE `Parentage application` HAS
    `a joint application by both parents`                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the other parent cannot join`                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Registrar satisfied the other parent does not dispute it`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `a court order or finding of parentage`                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `a presumption of parentage the Registrar may make under any law`          IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 18(1)
GIVEN app IS A `Parentage application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `both parents' identity may be registered` app IF
        "The Registrar is not to include information about the identity of a child's parents in the Register unless --"
    ..  "(a)"      ... app's `a joint application by both parents`
    ..  "(b)"      ... app's `the other parent cannot join`
    ..  "(c)"      ... app's `the Registrar satisfied the other parent does not dispute it`
    ..  "(d), (e)" ... app's `a court order or finding of parentage`
    OR  "(f)"      ... app's `a presumption of parentage the Registrar may make under any law`

-- "is not to include ... unless": a prohibition on the Registrar that lifts when a limb is met. Lifted, it is a permission ("may
-- include", s 18(2)(a)), not a duty.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 18(1), (2)(a)
GIVEN app IS A `Parentage application`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 18 -- the Registrar is not to include the parents' identity unless` app MEANS
    IF   `both parents' identity may be registered` app
    THEN PARTY `the Registrar`
         MAY   `include information about the identity of the child's parents in the Register`
    ELSE PARTY `the Registrar`
         SHANT `include information about the identity of the child's parents in the Register`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST  BREACH BY `the Registrar`
               BECAUSE "s 18(1): the Registrar included the parents' identity when no limb of s 18(1)(a)-(f) was met"

§§ `19(3), (4) -- a different surname after registration`

-- s 19(3): a proposed surname may be entered (a) on a joint application by both parents, or (b) on one parent's application if the
-- other parent has died, or cannot be found but other children of the same parents are registered in that surname. (4): only if
-- the surname "is not a prohibited name" and, if the child is 12 or more, the child consents or cannot understand the change.
DECLARE `Surname application` HAS
    `made jointly by both parents`                                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `the child's other parent dead`                                                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the other parent not found, and other children of the parents registered in the surname` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the proposed surname a prohibited name`                                                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the child's age in years`                                                                 IS A NUMBER
    `the child consenting, or unable to understand the change`                                 IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 19(3), (4)
GIVEN app IS A `Surname application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Registrar may enter the proposed surname` app IF
        (   "(3)(a)"     ... app's `made jointly by both parents`
         .. "(3)(b)(i)"  ... app's `the child's other parent dead`
         OR "(3)(b)(ii)" ... app's `the other parent not found, and other children of the parents registered in the surname`)
    AND "(4)(a)" ... NOT (app's `the proposed surname a prohibited name`)
    AND "(4)(b)" ... ((app's `the child's age in years` LESS THAN 12) OR app's `the child consenting, or unable to understand the change`)

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 19(3), (4)
GIVEN app IS A `Surname application`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 19(3) -- the Registrar may change the surname` app MEANS
    IF   `the Registrar may enter the proposed surname` app
    THEN PARTY `the Registrar`
         MAY   `change the surname entered in the Register to the proposed surname`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `22 -- the child's name`

-- s 22(1): the statement "must state the name of the child"; (2) not required for a stillborn child or a neonatal death (death of a
-- liveborn child within 28 days, s 4). (3): "The name is a matter of choice" -- no requirement of both a surname and a given name, nor
-- "the surname be the same as that of a parent". (4): the Registrar may assign a name if (a) the name stated is a prohibited name, or
-- (b) both parents lodged and "are unable to agree on the child's name".
--
-- (1) is a requirement on what the statement contains, not a duty with a party and a period: a condition (entry 5.2).
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 22(1), (2)
GIVEN b IS A Birth
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement must name the child` b IF
        NOT (b's `a stillbirth`)
    AND NOT (b's `a neonatal death`)

DECLARE Naming HAS
    `the name stated a prohibited name`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `lodged by both parents, who are unable to agree on it`    IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 22(4)
GIVEN n IS A Naming
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Registrar may assign a name` n IF
        "(a)" ... n's `the name stated a prohibited name`
    OR  "(b)" ... n's `lodged by both parents, who are unable to agree on it`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 22(4)
GIVEN n IS A Naming
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 22(4) -- the Registrar may assign a name to the child` n MEANS
    IF   `the Registrar may assign a name` n
    THEN PARTY `the Registrar`
         MAY   `assign a name to the child`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§ `Part 6 -- registration of marriages`

-- s 37: "If a marriage is solemnized in the State, the marriage must be registered under this Act." s 38: by lodging "a certificate of
-- the marriage under the Marriage Act 1961 of the Commonwealth" or, before that Act, the evidence the Registrar requires. Note to s
-- 38: "Under section 50(4) of the Marriage Act 1961 ... the authorised celebrant is responsible for lodging the certificate". s 39:
-- registered by including the certificate, or its particulars, in the Register. Part 6 sets no time limit.
--
-- s 37 names no party (entry 5.2), and s 38 says "A person MAY have a marriage registered": nobody in this Act owes the lodgement. The
-- celebrant's duty is the Commonwealth Act's, which is not encoded, so asking who must lodge it is answered `not covered`.
DECLARE `Marriage document` IS ONE OF
    `a certificate under the Marriage Act 1961`
    `the evidence the Registrar requires`

DECLARE Marriage HAS
    `solemnized in the State`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `solemnized before the Marriage Act 1961 commenced`    IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 37
GIVEN m IS A Marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the marriage must be registered` m IF m's `solemnized in the State`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 38
GIVEN m IS A Marriage
GIVETH A `Marriage document`
`what is lodged to register the marriage` m MEANS
    IF   m's `solemnized before the Marriage Act 1961 commenced`
    THEN `the evidence the Registrar requires`
    ELSE `a certificate under the Marriage Act 1961`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 38, note -- Marriage Act 1961 (Cth) s 50(4)
GIVEN m IS A Marriage
GIVETH AN `Answer` (DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`)
`who must lodge the marriage certificate, and by when` m MEANS
    `not covered` `the celebrant's duty to lodge the certificate under the Marriage Act 1961 (Cth) s 50(4) is not encoded in this model`

§ `Part 7 -- registration of deaths`

§§ `40 -- when a death must or may be registered`

-- s 40(1): "If a person dies in the State, the death must be registered under this Act." (2): also on a State court's or coroner's
-- order; (7): "The death of a stillborn child must be registered". (4), (5): a death on a flight or voyage to the State, or abroad of
-- a person domiciled or ordinarily resident in the State or leaving property here, MAY be registered; (6) the Registrar may refuse if
-- registered under a corresponding law. Like s 13(1), "must be registered" names no party (entry 5.2).
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 40(1), (2), (7)
GIVEN d IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the death must be registered` d IF
        "(1)" ... d's `in the State`
    ..  "(2)" ... d's `registration ordered by a State court or coroner`
    OR  "(7)" ... d's `the death of a stillborn child`

§§ `42 -- who notifies the death, and by when`

-- s 42: if a person dies in the State, "(a) the funeral director or other person who arranges for the disposal of the person's
-- remains; or (b) the person who is in charge of an educational or scientific institution where the person's remains are placed for
-- the purpose of medical education or research, must, within 14 days of the date of death, finding of the person's body or placement
-- of the person's body" -- (c) lodge a death registration statement and (d) give the Registrar the s 44 certificate. Penalty: $1 000.
--
-- The trace's AT 0 is whichever of the three dates "is relevant to the case"; choosing it is the caller's.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 42(a), (b)
GIVEN d IS A Death
GIVETH A `A party under this Act`
`who must notify the Registrar of the death` d MEANS
    IF   d's `the remains placed for medical education or research`
    THEN `the person in charge of the institution`
    ELSE `the funeral director or other person arranging disposal`

-- s 42(d) asks for "the certificate of cause of death provided under section 44", and s 44(5) says no such certificate need be given
-- where the doctor must report the death to a coroner. What the notifier gives the Registrar then is a matter for the Coroners Act
-- 1996, which is not encoded. A suspected death established at an inquest (s 43) is not encoded either.
--
-- (c) and (d) are two acts owed by the same party within the same 14 days: RAND, each with its own reason. This is the duty as s 42
-- states it, the rule a #TRACE runs; the cases this encoding does not cover are the next rule's.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 42
GIVEN d IS A Death
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 42 -- the notifier must lodge the statement and give the certificate within 14 days` d MEANS
    IF   NOT (d's `in the State`)
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE
                (PARTY  `who must notify the Registrar of the death` d
                 MUST   `lodge a death registration statement`
                 WITHIN 14
                 HENCE  FULFILLED
                 LEST   BREACH BY (`who must notify the Registrar of the death` d)
                        BECAUSE "s 42(c): the death registration statement was not lodged within 14 days")
            RAND
                (PARTY  `who must notify the Registrar of the death` d
                 MUST   `give the Registrar the certificate of cause of death`
                 WITHIN 14
                 HENCE  FULFILLED
                 LEST   BREACH BY (`who must notify the Registrar of the death` d)
                        BECAUSE "s 42(d): the certificate of cause of death was not given to the Registrar within 14 days")

-- THE QUESTION. The s 42 duty for this death, or why this encoding does not cover it: an event before 14 April 1999 (s 2), a
-- suspected death established at an inquest (s 43), or a death reportable to a coroner (s 42(d) with s 44(5)), tried in that order.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 2, s 42, s 43, s 44(5)
GIVEN d IS A Death
GIVETH AN `Answer` (DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`)
`s 42 -- the duty to notify the death, or why it is not covered` d MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF   `the event was before the Act was in operation` (d's `date of the death`)
        THEN `not covered` `a registrable event before 14 April 1999 is governed by the law then in force, which is not encoded in this model`
        IF   NOT (d's `in the State`)
        THEN `answered` (`s 42 -- the notifier must lodge the statement and give the certificate within 14 days` d)
        IF   d's `a suspected death established at an inquest`
        THEN `not covered` `a suspected death established at an inquest, where the coroner is the notifying person under s 43, is not encoded in this model`
        IF   d's `reportable to a coroner under section 17(3) of the Coroners Act 1996`
        THEN `not covered` `what s 42(d) requires for a death reportable to a coroner, which s 44(5) exempts from the certificate, is not encoded in this model`
        OTHERWISE `answered` (`s 42 -- the notifier must lodge the statement and give the certificate within 14 days` d)

-- The last day of the 14, as a date, for a case whose relevant date is the date of death.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 42
GIVEN d IS A Death
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day for notifying the death` d MEANS
    Date (Day (d's `date of the death`) PLUS 14)

§§ `45, 47 -- disposal of the remains`

-- s 45: the funeral director or other person arranging disposal (other than removal from the State) "must within 7 days after the
-- disposal of the remains, give the Registrar notice". s 47: if remains "have not been disposed of within 30 days after the date of
-- death", the person with custody must give notice. Penalty for each: $1 000.
-- The trace's AT 0 is the day of the disposal. s 45 itself excepts a disposal "by way of removal of the remains from the State", so
-- on its own words it puts no duty on anyone for a removal: FULFILLED. The removal's own notices are s 46's, which is not encoded,
-- so the question (the next rule) answers `not covered` for it.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 45
GIVEN `the disposal` IS A Disposal
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 45 -- the funeral director must give notice of the disposal within 7 days` `the disposal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the disposal`
    WHEN `removal of the remains from the State` THEN FULFILLED
    OTHERWISE
        PARTY  `the funeral director or other person arranging disposal`
        MUST   `give the Registrar notice of the disposal`
        WITHIN 7
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH BY `the funeral director or other person arranging disposal`
               BECAUSE "s 45: notice of the disposal was not given to the Registrar within 7 days after the disposal"

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 45, s 46
GIVEN `the disposal` IS A Disposal
GIVETH AN `Answer` (DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`)
`s 45 -- the duty to notify the disposal, or why it is not covered` `the disposal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the disposal`
    WHEN `removal of the remains from the State` THEN `not covered` `notice of a removal of remains from the State under s 46 is not encoded in this model`
    OTHERWISE `answered` (`s 45 -- the funeral director must give notice of the disposal within 7 days` `the disposal`)

-- Whether the remains "have not been disposed of within 30 days after the date of death" is a fact about dates (entry 4.4), asked as
-- at a day. The disposal is "if any" (entry 11.3): a MAYBE DATE.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 47
GIVEN d IS A Death
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a s 47 notice is due` d `the day asked about` IF
        Day `the day asked about` GREATER THAN `the thirtieth day`
    AND CONSIDER d's `date of the disposal`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
        WHEN JUST x  THEN Day x GREATER THAN `the thirtieth day`
    WHERE
        `the thirtieth day` MEANS Day (d's `date of the death`) PLUS 30

-- s 47 states no period for the notice, so the duty carries no WITHIN and stands until performed (entry 5.9).
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 47
GIVEN d IS A Death
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 47 -- the custodian must give notice that the remains have not been disposed of` d `the day asked about` MEANS
    IF   `a s 47 notice is due` d `the day asked about`
    THEN PARTY `the funeral director or other person with custody of the remains`
         MUST  `give the Registrar notice that the remains have not been disposed of`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST  BREACH BY `the funeral director or other person with custody of the remains`
               BECAUSE "s 47: the person with custody did not give the Registrar notice that the remains had not been disposed of within 30 days"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `48 -- the Registrar registers`

-- s 48(1): "The Registrar is to register a death by making an entry about the death in the Register"; (2) on incomplete particulars;
-- (3) even though an inquest or coronial investigation has not found the cause. The funeral director notifies (s 42); the Registrar
-- registers (s 48). Part 7 sets no time limit on the Registrar, so the duty carries no WITHIN.
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 48(1)
GIVEN `the candidate` IS A `A party under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `registers the death` `the candidate` IF
    `the candidate` EQUALS `the Registrar`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 48(1), (3)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under this Act` `An act under this Act`
`s 48(1) -- the Registrar is to register the death` MEANS
    PARTY `the Registrar`
    MUST  `register the death`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the Registrar`
          BECAUSE "s 48(1): the Registrar did not register the death"

-- s 48(4): "A death certificate issued before the completion of an inquest or other coronial investigation into the cause of death
-- must be endorsed" to indicate that fact. Passive, and about what a certificate must carry: a condition (entry 5.2).
@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) s 48(4)
GIVEN d IS A Death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the death certificate must be endorsed` d IF
    d's `a coronial investigation into the cause incomplete`

§ `Penalties`

-- ss 12(2), 16(1), 42, 44, 45 and 47: "Penalty: $1 000." for each. A penalty is a CEILING on the court's sentence, not an amount owed
-- (the phrasebook, entry 5.7).
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `late birth notice under s 12`
    `late birth registration under s 16`
    `late death notification under s 42`
    `late cause of death certificate under s 44`
    `late disposal notice under s 45`
    `no notice of undisposed remains under s 47`

@ref Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (WA) ss 12(2), 16(1), 42, 44, 45, 47
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum penalty in dollars` `the offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `late birth notice under s 12`                THEN 1000
    WHEN `late birth registration under s 16`          THEN 1000
    WHEN `late death notification under s 42`          THEN 1000
    WHEN `late cause of death certificate under s 44`  THEN 1000
    WHEN `late disposal notice under s 45`             THEN 1000
    WHEN `no notice of undisposed remains under s 47`  THEN 1000
